%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8145] ## **Vibrating masses**
%[text] ### **Physical system and equations of motion**
%[text] Consider a system of $n${"altText":"n"} masses connected by strings with uniform tension $T${"altText":"T"} and horizontal distance $l${"altText":"l"} between each mass.
%[text] 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Block diagram of tension masses](text:image:84b7)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *System of equally spaced connected masses*
%[text] Each mass has a mass $m\_i${"altText":"m\_i"} and a vertical displacement $x\_i${"altText":"x\_i"} from a reference position. For simplicity, masses are assumed to be displaced only in the vertical direction. You can draw a free body diagram for $i^{th}${"altText":"i^(th)"} mass and use it to derive the equations of motion.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Free body diagram for a given mass](text:image:4d3d)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Free body diagram for the* $i^{th}${"altText":"i^(th)"} *mass*
%[text] The angle $\\theta${"altText":"theta"} can be related to the displacements. Note that in this calculation the displacement is assumed to be small, so that the horizontal distance between the masses $\\approx l${"altText":"approximately l"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\sin \\theta = \\frac{x\_i - x\_{i-1}}{l}${"altText":"sin theta = (x\_i - x\_(i-1))\/(l)"}
%[text] The force acting in the $y${"altText":"y"}-direction is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{rl} T\_y &= T \\sin \\theta \\\\ &= \\frac{T}{l} (x\_i - x\_{i-1}) \\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: T\_y  = T sin theta ;  = (T)\/(l) (x\_i - x\_(i-1))"}
%[text] Performing the same analysis on the other side of the mass yields a similar result. Applying Newton's second law yields:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{rl}\nm\_i \\ddot{x}\_i &= \\frac{T}{l}( - x\_i + x\_{i-1} )  + \\frac{T}{l}(x\_{i+1} - x\_{i} ) \n\\\\\nm\_i \\ddot{x}\_i &= \\frac{T}{l}( x\_{i-1} - 2x\_i + x\_{i+1} )\n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: m\_i second derivative(x)\_i  = (T)\/(l)( - x\_i + x\_(i-1) ) + (T)\/(l)(x\_(i+1) - x\_(i) ) ; m\_i second derivative(x)\_i  = (T)\/(l)( x\_(i-1) - 2x\_i + x\_(i+1) )"}
%[text] Note that the double dot notation represents the second derivative with respect to time: $\\ddot{x}\_i = \\frac{d^2 x\_i}{dt^2}${"altText":"second derivative(x)\_i = (d^2 x\_i)\/(dt^2)"}.
%[text] For simplicity, assume $m\_i = T = l = 1${"altText":"m\_i = T = l = 1"}, yielding:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{rl}\n\\ddot{x}\_i &=  x\_{i-1} - 2x\_i + x\_{i+1} \n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: second derivative(x)\_i  = x\_(i-1) - 2x\_i + x\_(i+1)"}
%[text] for each mass. Note that for the first and last mass the equations are identical but lack the displacements of the missing neighbor:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{rl}\n\\ddot{x}\_1 &=  - 2x\_i + x\_{i+1} \n\\\\\n\\ddot{x}\_n &=  x\_{n-1} - 2x\_n \n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: second derivative(x)\_1  = - 2x\_i + x\_(i+1) ; second derivative(x)\_n  = x\_(n-1) - 2x\_n"}
%[text] ### **Matrix form of the equations of motion**
%[text] To represent this system in matrix form, define the vector of displacement variables:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{x} = \\left( \\matrix{ x\_1 \\cr x\_2 \\cr \\vdots \\cr x\_n } \\right)${"altText":"x = ( matrix \[ x\_1 ; x\_2 ; ... ; x\_n \] )"}.
%[text] Using this definition, you can write the equations of motion in the form:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\ddot{\\mathbf{x}} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}${"altText":"second derivative(x) = A x"}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:6118) **Task.** Define the number of masses in the system, `n` (you will use this value in the remainder of the example). 
n = 4;      % Number of masses %[control:slider:85be]{"position":[5,6]}
  %[control:button:4a8a]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:964b) **Exercise**. Based on the definition of $\\mathbf{x}${"altText":"x"} and the ODE system:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\begin{array}{rl}\n&\\ddot{x}\_1 =  - 2x\_i + x\_{i+1} \n\\\\\n&\\ddot{x}\_i =  x\_{i-1} - 2x\_i + x\_{i+1} \n\\\\\n&\\ddot{x}\_n =  x\_{n-1} - 2x\_n \n\\end{array}${"altText":"system of equations: , second derivative(x)\_1 = - 2x\_i + x\_(i+1) ; , second derivative(x)\_i = x\_(i-1) - 2x\_i + x\_(i+1) ; , second derivative(x)\_n = x\_(n-1) - 2x\_n"}
%[text] what will the form of the coefficient matrix $\\mathbf{A}${"altText":"A"} be in the matrix form of the ODE system, $\\ddot{\\mathbf{x}} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}${"altText":"second derivative(x) = A x"}?
disp("Did you solve the problem?")  % Use this dropdown to check your work %[control:dropdown:5994]{"position":[1,35]}
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:65eb) **Exercise**. Define the coefficient matrix $\\mathbf{A${"altText":"A"} in MATLAB as a numeric matrix for an arbitrary number of masses $n${"altText":"n"}.
%[text] **Hint**: Use the [`diag`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/diag.html) function three times to create three diagonal matrices and sum the result with the correct coefficients. The size of the system is stored in the variable `n`.
% Define the matrix A

%%
%[text] ### **Solution to the ODE system in MATLAB**
%[text] The ODE system 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\ddot{\\mathbf{x}} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}${"altText":"second derivative(x) = A x"}
%[text] is solved here using a numeric ODE solver. In this process, the ODE is integrated to yield a solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)${"altText":"x(t)"} given initial conditions $\\mathbf{x}(0)${"altText":"x(0)"} and $\\dot\\mathbf{x}(0)${"altText":"first derivative(x)(0)"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:50ea) **Task.** Run the section to see the solution. Notice how compact the ODE implementation is by examining the function ODEStringMasses. Matrix representations, like this one, are effective for writing concise code.
x01 = 0.2;   % Initial displacement of the first mass %[control:slider:2534]{"position":[7,10]}
tf =10;     % Duration of the simulation %[control:slider:8051]{"position":[5,7]}
  %[control:button:049f]{"position":[1,2]}

x0 = zeros(n,1);
x0(1) = x01;
tlim = [0 tf]; 
[t,x,xdot] = SolveMassesODE(x0,tlim,n);

figure("Position",[100 200 1000 400])
plot(t,x)
xlabel("t")
ylabel("x(t)")
legend("x_{" + strsplit(num2str(1:n)) + "}","Location","EastOutside")
%[text] ![](text:image:6041) **Try**. 
%[text] - Adjust the number of masses `n` above and rerun the simulation. Does the natural oscillation frequency change?
%[text] - Adjust the initial displacement and duration of the simulation. What happens as $t${"altText":"t"} becomes large? \
%%
%[text] #### **Visualize the motion**
%[text] The graph of displacements can be difficult to relate to physical experience. Run this section to visualize the motion.
x01 = 0.35;   % Initial displacement of the first mass %[control:slider:9ccd]{"position":[7,11]}
tf =10;     % Duration of the simulation %[control:slider:99aa]{"position":[5,7]}
Nframes = 110; % Adjust the number of frames to speed up or slow down the animation %[control:slider:5605]{"position":[11,14]}
  %[control:button:6954]{"position":[1,2]}

x0 = zeros(n,1);
x0(1) = x01;
tlim = [0 tf]; 
[t,x,xdot] = SolveMassesODE(x0,tlim,n); 
AnimateODEMasses(t,x,Nframes)
%%
%[text] ### **Eigenvectors and natural oscillation modes**
%[text] The natural modes of oscillation of the connected mass system can be identified by computing the eigenvectors of the coefficient matrix `A`.
% The coefficient matrix
A = diag(ones(n-1,1),-1) - 2*diag(ones(n,1)) + diag(ones(n-1,1),1)
%[text] ![](text:image:279a) **Exercise**. Compute and visualize the eigenvectors of the coefficient matrix.
%[text] ![](text:image:308b) **Task**. Compute the eigen decomposition of $\\mathbf{A${"altText":"A"} in MATLAB. Store the outputs in `V` and `D`.

%%
%[text] ![](text:image:7881) **Try**. Each eigenvector represents the displacements of the masses according to a natural vibration mode. This section visualizes the eigenvectors with the masses shown. Adjust the mode index to view the natural vibrational modes.
ModeIndex = 1; % The mode index must be <= the number of masses %[control:slider:440f]{"position":[13,14]}
VisualizeMasses(ModeIndex,n)
%[text] 
%[text] ![](text:image:82b4) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - Based on the simulation output, which mode do you think is dominant in the simulation generated above? \
%%
%[text] #### **Computation of the modes present in the ODE solution**
%[text] The eigenvectors provide an alternative basis for the motion of masses. You can compute which modes are present in the simulation by expressing the solution in terms of the eigenvector basis:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{x} = b\_1 \\mathbf{v}\_1 + b\_2 \\mathbf{v}\_2 + b\_3 \\mathbf{v}\_3 + \\ldots + b\_n \\mathbf{v}\_n${"altText":"x = b\_1 v\_1 + b\_2 v\_2 + b\_3 v\_3 + ... + b\_n v\_n"}
%[text] The coefficients $\\mathbf{b}${"altText":"b"} determine the weights of each mode.
%[text] In matrix form, this linear combination is:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{V} \\mathbf{b}${"altText":"x = V b"}
%[text] where $\\mathbf{V}${"altText":"V"} is the eigenvector matrix in the eigen decomposition. To solve for the mode coefficients at any time $t${"altText":"t"}, you can compute the matrix solution for $\\mathbf{b}${"altText":"b"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:3aff) **Exercise**. Solve for the mode coefficients $\\mathbf{b}${"altText":"b"} at the last time step in the simulation. 
%[text] **Hint**: To access the values of $\\mathbf{x}${"altText":"x"} in the last time step, use:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] x(end,:)'
%[text] ```


%%
%[text] ![](text:image:20a5) **Try**. Run this section to plot the mode coefficients over time in the solution. The mode coefficients are computed at each time step below.
figure("Position",[100 200 1000 400]) 
b = zeros(size(x));
[V,~] = eig(A);
for k = 1:length(t)
    b(k,:) = V\x(k,:)'; % Compute mode coefficients at each time step
end
plot(t,b) % Plot b here
legend("b_{" + strsplit(num2str(1:n)) + "}","Location","EastOutside") % This adds a legend to the plot
%[text] ![](text:image:7339) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - Does there appear to be a dominant mode present in this particular solution? There may not be.
%[text] - Do different modes have different periods of oscillation? \
%%
%[text] ### **Eigenmode solutions**
%[text] In the above sections, you analyzed a solution using eigenvectors. It is also possible to use the eigenvector basis to construct the initial condition for the model. To do so, you can set the initial displacement of the system to a sum of the natural modes:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\mathbf{x}\_0 = b\_1 \\mathbf{v}\_1 + b\_2 \\mathbf{v}\_2 + b\_3 \\mathbf{v}\_3 + \\ldots + b\_n \\mathbf{v}\_n${"altText":"x\_0 = b\_1 v\_1 + b\_2 v\_2 + b\_3 v\_3 + ... + b\_n v\_n"}
%[text] The system will continue to oscillate with those natural modes indefinitely as there is no damping term in the equation. 
%[text] ![](text:image:1855) **Task**. Define a new $n${"altText":"n"}-element mode coefficient vector `b` to determine which modes will be present in the simulation. You can vary the values of `b` to emphasize different solution modes.


%[text] ![](text:image:5ab7) **Task**. Compute the initial condition vector `x0` based on the coefficient vector `b` and the eigenvector matrix `V`.

%%
%[text] #### **Visualize the motion**
%[text] Check the box to run this section, compute the  ODE solution, and visualize the solution.
tf =20;     % Duration of the simulation %[control:slider:3c1a]{"position":[5,7]}
Nframes = 130; % Adjust the number of frames to speed up or slow down the animation %[control:slider:31cb]{"position":[11,14]}
  %[control:button:67db]{"position":[1,2]}

tlim = [0 tf]; 
[t,x,xdot] = SolveMassesODE(x0,tlim,n);
AnimateODEMasses(t,x,Nframes)
%[text] ![](text:image:305f) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - Can you recognize each eigenmode individually?
%[text] - What happens when you change the relative scale factors (i.e., the eigenvector coefficients $b\_i${"altText":"b\_i"}) for different eigenmodes?
%[text] - Is energy transferred between eigenmodes?
%[text] - Go back and alter the coefficient vector $\\mathbf{b}${"altText":"b"} so that the initial condition $\\mathbf{x}\_0${"altText":"x\_0"} contains different modes. Are the changes reflected in the dynamic behavior? \
